<prefabs>
	<!-- Functional howdah platform for the TAOM war elephant: floor, rails and crew frames, invisible in game (every
	     mesh is visible_only_when_editing). It is fitted to the elite howdah harness mesh (sk_hd_elep_armor_howdah_elite_a;
	     the heavy one shares its deck), which is skinned to the elephant's spine. The HorseHarness sk_elephant_armor_howdah_elite
	     shows that mesh and is the Harad elephant rider's harness; it gets this platform and a crew. The plain
	     sk_elephant_armor_a still gets the platform, without crew (HowdahHarness, #627).

	     Rebuilt 2026-09-18 on the moving-platform pattern of the vanilla siege tower (Native props_siege_siegetowers.xml)
	     and the War Sails ships, read as a design reference only (TAOM does not require the DLC):
	     docs/features/elephant/howdah-ship-research-2026-09-18.md. The first version was a port of ADOD_Beasts's 4-seat
	     howdah (provenance: docs/reference/provenance-register.md).

	     SCALE (2026-09-22). The elephant is 1.3x since then (taom_war_elephant body_length 130, Mike), and this
	     prefab is authored at that FINAL size: every transform below is the 1.0x value times 1.3, and the root
	     sits at 3.2 x 1.3 = 4.16 m (ElephantConfig.AuthoredScale). The lengths quoted in the rest of this header
	     are the 1.0x MEASUREMENTS off the FBX, kept exactly as measured; multiply by 1.3 for the running game.
	     Deck top 4.095 m; the body capsule top rises from 2.7 to 3.51 m and the floor underside clears it by
	     about 0.56 m. Nothing scales this at runtime: a runtime-scaled entity carrying a physics body is what
	     dropped the mumakil's whole crew (#627). HowdahPrefabTests pins body_length to the constant.

	     FRAME. TaomHowdahMachine places this root at the elephant's origin + ElephantConfig.HowdahHeightAboveGround
	     (3.2 m), facing the elephant: +y forward, +x right, z up from 3.2 m. The elite howdah's deck, re-measured from
	     SK_Elephant_Armor_Variations.fbx on 2026-09-19 by the true vertex extents of its upward faces at 3.15 m, spans
	     x -0.68 to 0.68 and y -2.026 to -0.270: 1.36 m across, 1.756 m along, centred 1.148 m behind the origin. The
	     first build read 0.805 m from face CENTRES instead, which put the platform 0.34 m too far forward and left the
	     front pair of archers standing out past the front wall in the first crew test (Mike, 2026-09-19). Every child
	     is now placed around (0, -1.148), floor top at z -0.05. The machine leaves its pilot,
	     ammo and wait tags at the engine defaults ("Pilot", "ammopickup", "Wait"), which match no child here.

	     PHYSICS. One body: the floor, bo_empire_keep_a_door_top (bbox x -0.81..0.80, y -0.89..0.87, z -0.06..0.32)
	     scaled to the deck and to 0.02 m thick (was 0.12 until 2026-09-22: the same body under the mumakil's decks
	     stood archers on its raised ends, and squashing it took every one back onto the deck; top unchanged), flagged moveable like every vanilla moving platform (the entity is
	     re-framed every tick). Its underside stays 0.33 m above the elephant's body capsule (top 2.7 m), since a body
	     inside that capsule is the leading suspect for the elephant slide. It is ALSO flagged barrier, which is in
	     BodyFlags.CommonCollisionExcludeFlagsForMissile: without that, an archer shooting at anything below the deck
	     puts the arrow into its own platform.

	     The four bo_barrier rails were DELETED 2026-09-20. They were the siege tower's railing, meant to keep agents
	     aboard, and the seat's per-frame teleport already does that, so they held nobody. What they could still do is
	     stand chest-high in front of a drawn bow: missiles ignore a barrier body, but the mask that a clear-shot check
	     would use (CommonCollisionExcludeFlagsForCombat) does not, and no managed code reads it, so it is unverifiable
	     from the decompile. The archers draw to 85 percent and re-nock forever, so the rails go until they shoot. The
	     harness's own rim is the visual wall, and it carries no collision.

	     CREW. TWO frames tagged taom_howdah_crew, in a line at (0, -0.850) and (0, -1.630), 0.78 m apart. Crew count
	     is limited by CAPSULE SPACING, not by the deck: a human capsule is 0.37 m in radius, so frames closer than
	     0.74 m make their occupants shove each other every frame, the seat teleports them back, and the engine reads
	     tens of m/s of movement, which is what kept the crew from ever loosing an arrow. Abreast is impossible here
	     (the interior is 1.26 m across, two capsules need 1.48), so the deck's 1.48 m length holds exactly two. A
	     bigger platform, or the mumakil's multiple decks, can hold more on the same rule. The deck is 1.36 m across but
	     the WALLS, measured 2026-09-19 from the vertical faces between 3.25 and 3.85 m, stand at x +-0.63 inside and
	     y -1.98 to -0.50, so the interior is 1.26 x 1.48 m and the deck sticks out about 0.17 m ahead of the front
	     wall. Two 0.37 m capsules side by side need 1.48 m of width and there is 1.26, so a four-frame layout puts a
	     body through a wall: with four, the front pair's capsules reached y -0.43, past the front wall's inner face at
	     -0.50 (Mike saw them standing out of the howdah, twice). Two fit in a line, 0.78 m between centres, which is the
	     spacing two 0.37 m capsules need.
	     A frame carries AutoSheathWeapons="false" and nothing else, but note that the engine never reads it here:
	     StandingPoint.TickAux is the only consumer and it returns unless HasUser, which needs UserAgent, which only
	     UsableMissionObject.OnUse sets, and this seat deliberately does not call it. The bow stays out because nothing
	     sheathes it. The variable stays because it becomes live the day anyone routes seating through base.OnUse.
	     The seat keeps each archer in its formation for the whole battle (a null formation pins
	     Agent.MissileRangeAdjusted at 0f, which is why the first crew tests drew bows and never shot) and holds it in
	     place with a scripted position rather than a detachment. The machine's NavMeshPrefabName names a deck navmesh
	     if one is ever exported; empty is what the status line reports as nav=0, and 0 is a walkable face id anyway.
	     LOCATION AND NAME. Lives in LOTRLOME_Armory/Prefabs with the elephant's Monster, meshes and clips (moved from the
	     TAOM module 2026-09-18). Renamed from taom_howdah_agent 2026-09-19: TAOM installs from v2.0.22 to v2.0.30 keep a
	     copy under that name in Modules/TAOM/Prefabs, and two prefabs of one name have no defined winner.
	     ElephantConfig.HowdahPrefabName is the name the C# instantiates. The Armory is unversioned: the repo keeps a
	     byte-identical snapshot at docs/reference/lotrlome-armory-snapshot/Prefabs/taom_howdah_platform.xml, and
	     TAOM.Tests/Features/Elephant/HowdahPrefabTests.cs pins the structure and checks the live copy matches it. -->
	<game_entity name="taom_howdah_platform" old_prefab_name="" mobility="1">
		<flags>
			<flag name="record_to_scene_replay" value="true"/>
		</flags>
		<visibility_masks>
			<visibility_mask name="visible_only_when_editing" value="true"/>
		</visibility_masks>
		<transform position="0.000, 0.000, 0.000" rotation_euler="0.000, 0.000, 0.000"/>
		<scripts>
			<script name="TaomHowdahMachine">
				<variables>
					<variable name="NavMeshPrefabName" value=""/>
				</variables>
			</script>
		</scripts>
		<children>
			<!-- Floor: top at z -0.065 (4.095 m), the measured deck 1.36 x 1.756 m at 1.0x (1.77 x 2.28 m here), 0.025 m thick. -->
			<game_entity name="howdah_floor" old_prefab_name="" mobility="1">
				<visibility_masks>
					<visibility_mask name="visible_only_when_editing" value="true"/>
				</visibility_masks>
				<transform position="0.005, -1.479, -0.086" rotation_euler="0.000, 0.000, 0.000" scale="1.099, 1.297, 0.065"/>
				<physics shape="bo_empire_keep_a_door_top">
					<body_flags>
						<body_flag name="moveable"/>
						<body_flag name="barrier"/>
						<body_flag name="dont_collide_with_camera"/>
					</body_flags>
				</physics>
				<components>
					<meta_mesh_component name="empire_keep_a_door_top"/>
				</components>
			</game_entity>
			<!-- Crew frames: TWO, one behind the other, 1.01 m apart at 1.3x (0.78 m at 1.0x; 2026-09-20, Mike). The spacing is PHYSICAL,
			     not visual: a human body capsule is 0.37 m in radius, so two closer than 0.74 m overlap and shove
			     each other every frame, the seat teleports them back, and the engine reads that as tens of m/s,
			     which is what stopped the crew shooting all along. The walls are cosmetic (the rails are gone and
			     the harness mesh has no collision), so 2 cm of capsule past the rim is fine and crowding is not. -->
			<game_entity name="howdah_crew_front" old_prefab_name="" mobility="1">
				<visibility_masks>
					<visibility_mask name="visible_only_when_editing" value="true"/>
				</visibility_masks>
				<tags>
					<tag name="taom_howdah_crew"/>
				</tags>
				<transform position="0.000, -1.105, -0.065" rotation_euler="0.000, 0.000, 0.000"/>
				<components>
					<meta_mesh_component name="arrow_new_icon"/>
				</components>
				<scripts>
					<script name="TaomHowdahStandingPoint">
						<variables>
							<variable name="AutoSheathWeapons" value="false"/>
						</variables>
					</script>
				</scripts>
			</game_entity>
			<game_entity name="howdah_crew_back" old_prefab_name="" mobility="1">
				<visibility_masks>
					<visibility_mask name="visible_only_when_editing" value="true"/>
				</visibility_masks>
				<tags>
					<tag name="taom_howdah_crew"/>
				</tags>
				<transform position="0.000, -2.119, -0.065" rotation_euler="0.000, 0.000, 0.000"/>
				<components>
					<meta_mesh_component name="arrow_new_icon"/>
				</components>
				<scripts>
					<script name="TaomHowdahStandingPoint">
						<variables>
							<variable name="AutoSheathWeapons" value="false"/>
						</variables>
					</script>
				</scripts>
			</game_entity>
		</children>
	</game_entity>
</prefabs>
